A||||4||mrfixit58||can a 3300 be turned into a 3400???||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||02-29-2000||08:28 AM||||I was looking around at the local U-Pull-It yard the other day and spotted a 3300 motor in a FWD Buick and it got me thinking.... Is this the same block as the 2.8 with a larger bore and stroke just like the 3.4? If so, will all the Fiero accessaries and covers bolt up to the block? Will the Fiero heads work?<P>My thought was, if I've got to rebuild my 2.8, why not go to the 3300? Let's assume the crank is the same as the 3.4. I'd bore it 30-40 thousands over (or the equivalent to a 3.4) and install new pistons bolt on all the Fiero covers and heads and "viola" a 3.4 with the starter on the correct side (I think).<P>Has anyone looked into this?<P>Roy L.||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||02-29-2000||03:36 PM||||It's a completely different breed of cat. The 2.8/3.1/3.4 series is a Chevy 60 degree V6 engine where as the Buick 3.3 is an early forerunner of the Buick 3800 V6 engine. You might be able to fit the 3.3 into the Fiero, but forget about using most 2.8 parts on or in it. Good idea though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||02-29-2000||04:23 PM||||Well... ya never know unless ya ask!!<P>Thanks Batboy.||||reg||12
Z||000003||terryk||02-29-2000||08:02 PM||||You can still change a 3300 to a 3400. It just won't be the right 3400.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||02-29-2000||10:43 PM||||The 3800 fits into the bay with mods. There are install kits available. Therefore, the 3300 should use most of the same mods to fit.<P>I'd stay with the 3.4, though.||||reg||1
